<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jacob Baradaeus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 500–578</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jacob Baradaeus was a Syrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> who organised the Miaphysite church in opposition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-chalcedon">Council of Chalcedon</a>. Consecrated bishop of Edessa around 542 at the request of the Ghassanid king al-Harith, he travelled across the eastern provinces, often in disguise, ordaining many clergy and bishops. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a> was long called Jacobite after him.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jacob was born in the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamis-cyprus">Constantia</a>, near modern Viranşehir, the son of Theophilus bar Manu, a priest. At the age of two he was left in the care of Eustathius, abbot of the Monastery of Fsilta, where he studied Greek, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> and religious texts, and when his mother later tried to bring him home he refused. After his parents died he gave his inheritance to the poor and freed the slaves he had inherited. Ordained deacon and priest at the monastery, he became known as a miracle-worker.</p><p>His church grew out of the division that followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-chalcedon">Council of Chalcedon</a> in 451, and the persecution of its opponents under Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justin-i">Justin I</a> from 518 left them with few leaders. Empress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodora-wife-of-justinian-i">Theodora</a>, herself a non-Chalcedonian, invited Jacob to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, where he lived for 15 years in the Monastery of Sykai. When Ephraim, Patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, persecuted the non-Chalcedonians, Theodora and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-harith-ibn-jabala">Al-Harith ibn Jabalah</a>, King of the Ghassanids, urged Pope <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> to consecrate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, and Theodosius made Jacob bishop of Edessa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Jacob then ordained clergy across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. The Roman government tried to imprison him, and he travelled in disguise, which earned him the name Burde'ana, man in ragged clothes, the source of Baradaeus. He ordained Sergius of Tella as Patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> in 544 and Paul in 550, and consecrated Ahudemmeh as Metropolitan of the East in 559. After the Second Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> of 553 failed to win over the Syrian non-Chalcedonians, he began to form the separate church that became the Syriac Orthodox Church.</p><p>His later years were troubled by disputes. In 571 he approved an edict of union with the Chalcedonians, then withdrew his approval when many of his own side protested. When Pope Peter IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> deposed Paul as patriarch in 576, Jacob first denounced him and then accepted the deposition to reconcile the Syrian and Egyptian non-Chalcedonians, a compromise that set off violence between his supporters and Paul's in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Jacob left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> with several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, fell ill on the way at the Monastery of St. Romanus in Maiuma, and died there on 30 July 578. According to Cyriacus, Bishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardin">Mardin</a>, his remains stayed there until they were moved to the Monastery of Fsilta in 622. He is venerated as a saint in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oriental-orthodox-churches">Oriental Orthodox Churches</a>, with a feast day on 31 July.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jacob Baradaeus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Ghassanids moved between camps such as Jabiyah in the Golan, built churches and towers, and fervently supported Miaphysite Christianity, obtaining the consecration of <b>Jacob Baradaeus</b> as bishop.</blockquote><a href="/state/ghassanids">Ghassanids</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Antiochia in Mesopotamia, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in Edessa, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.23,39.76&amp;year=550" aria-label="Antiochia in Mesopotamia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Jacob Baradaeus"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5661 4355h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5661 4355h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.23,39.76&amp;year=550">What was here in 550 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
